WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
Tokenization.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.728
Before a language model reads anything, your text has to become numbers.

3
00:00:06.728 --> 00:00:08.867
This is the step that decides how.

4
00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:13.700
Tokenization splits text into units a model can number.

5
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.200
Each unit is looked up in a fixed vocabulary, and the model only sees the integer ID.

6
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.133
That vocabulary is frozen at training time.

7
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:25.025
Watch a log line get split.

8
00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:28.033
Oxygen and tank are common, so each stays whole.

9
00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:33.389
The rare word flatlined breaks into pieces, and every piece becomes an ID.

10
00:00:33.389 --> 00:00:35.233
These IDs are illustrative.

11
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:38.850
So where does the vocabulary come from?

12
00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.267
It's trained, using byte pair encoding.

13
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:43.167
Start with single characters.

14
00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:45.933
Count which neighboring pairs appear most often.

15
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:48.700
Merge the winning pair into one new token.

16
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.316
Repeat until the vocabulary is full.

17
00:00:51.316 --> 00:00:56.333
Nothing is ever out of vocabulary, because the worst case falls back to bytes.

18
00:00:57.533 --> 00:00:59.467
This one choice explains a lot.

19
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:04.900
Cost and context limits are measured in tokens, about three quarters of a word each.

20
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.667
Non-English text and code cost more tokens per character.

21
00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:12.067
And models can't count letters, because they never see them.

22
00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:14.733
Here's the idea in code.

23
00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:18.633
With tiktoken, we encode the log line and count the IDs.

24
00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:23.900
BERT's tokenizer gives a different count, because each model has its own vocabulary.

25
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.400
Here's why this matters.

26
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.767
Before embedding forty thousand log chunks, you tokenize a sample to size the job.

27
00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:39.600
The average chunk is about four twenty tokens, so chunking at five hundred words would blow past a five twelve token limit.

28
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.990
Every log gets truncated at the tail, where the resolution lives.

29
00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:45.500
Count tokens, not words.

30
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:47.604
Let's recap.

31
00:01:47.604 --> 00:01:52.500
Tokenization turns text into integer IDs from a fixed vocabulary.

32
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.067
Byte pair encoding builds that vocabulary by merging frequent pairs into subwords.

33
00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:02.400
Cost, limits, and chunking are all counted in tokens, not words.

34
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.279
And every model has its own tokenizer.

35
00:02:05.279 --> 00:02:06.500
Next up: stemming.
